WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:05.480
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.349
The better fluid simulation techniques out
there typically run on our graphical cards,

00:00:10.349 --> 00:00:15.300
which, if we formulate the problem in a way
that the many compute units within can do

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.939
in parallel, if they row in unison, if you
will, we'll be greeted by an incredible bump

00:00:20.939 --> 00:00:22.890
in the speed of the simulation.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:27.320
This leads to amazingly detailed simulations,
many of which you'll see in this footage.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.810
It's going to be really good!

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:35.409
However, sometimes we have a simulation domain
that is so large, it simply cannot be loaded

00:00:35.409 --> 00:00:37.940
into the memory of our graphical card.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:39.370
What about those problems?

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:45.070
Well, the solution could be subdividing the
problem into independent subdomains and solving

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.549
them separately on multiple devices.

00:00:47.549 --> 00:00:51.760
Slice the problem up into smaller, more manageable
pieces.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.400
Divide and conquer.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.220
But wait, we would just be pretending that
these subdomains are independent, because

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:03.000
in reality, they are clearly not, because
there is a large amount of fluid flowing between

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.670
them, and it takes quite a bit of algebraic
wizardry to make sure that the information

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:12.030
exchange between these devices happens correctly,
and on time.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:15.640
But if we do it correctly, we can see our
reward on the screen.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.930
Let's marvel at it together!

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.440
Oh, hohoho, yeah!

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:29.920
I cannot get tired of this.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:35.400
Typically, the simulation in the individual
subdomains are computed on one or more separate

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.250
graphical cards, and the administration on
the intersecting interface takes place on

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:41.460
the processor.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:46.380
The challenge of such a solution is that one
has to be able to show that the solution of

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:51.240
this problem formulation is equivalent to
solving the huge original problem.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:56.090
And, it also has to be significantly more
efficient to be useful for projects in the

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:57.090
industry.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:02.510
The paper is one of the finest pieces of craftsmanship
I've seen lately, the link is available in

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:03.770
the video description.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:07.930
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

